OBJECTIVES=[('12.1 Numerical solution of non-linear equations',['Describe importance of numerical methods.','Explain the basic principles of solving a non-linear equation in one variable.','Calculate real roots of a non-linear equation in one variable by bisection method, regula-falsi method and Newton-Raphson method.','Use MAPLE command fsolve to find numerical solution of an equation and demonstrate through examples.']),('12.2 Numerical quadrature',['Define numerical quadrature. Use trapezoidal rule and Simpson’s rule to compute approximate values of definite integrals without error terms.','Use MAPLE command trapezoid for trapezoidal rule and simpson for Simpson’s rule and demonstrate through examples.'])]
INTRO=[heading('Introduction'),paragraph('Scientists, economists, engineers, and other researchers study relationships between quantities. For example, an engineer may need to know how the illumination from a light source on an object is related to the distance between the object and the source; a biologist may wish to investigate how the population of a bacterial colony varies with time in the presence of a toxin; an economist may wish to determine the relationship between demand for a certain commodity and its market price. The mathematical study of such relationships involves the concept of non-linear equations. For example, the value of the assets of a certain company at time t years is modeled by a non-linear equation:'),eq(r'f(t)=100,000-75,000e^{-2t}'),paragraph('t is measured in years. The standing rule for solving non-linear equations algebraic is quadratic formula. In this case, it is not valid to obtain the actual number of t (years) at which the asset function f(t) is going to be zero. Now we are in position to obtain the approximate number of years t that can be found by using numerical procedures. In this unit, we learn numerical procedures recommended as bracketing methods and iterative methods.'),note('The quadratic formula applies to quadratics, not all nonlinear equations. This exponential example can be solved exactly with logarithms: f(t)=0 implies e^(−2t)=4/3 and t=−(1/2)ln(4/3)≈−0.143841 years. For future time t≥0, f(t)≥25000, so there is no future zero under the printed model. Numerical root searches must respect this domain rather than assume a future root exists.')]
ROOT_START=[heading('12.1 Numerical Solution of Non-linear Equations'),paragraph('Numerical analysis is the theory of constructive methods in mathematical analysis. A constructive method is in turn a procedure that permits us to obtain the solution of a mathematical problem with arbitrary precision in a finite number of steps that can be prepared rationally.'),heading('12.1.1 Importance of numerical methods'),paragraph('Numerical analysis is both a Science and an Art. As a science, it is concerned with the process by which a mathematical problem can be solved arithmetically. As an art, numerical analysis is concerned with choosing the procedure best suited to the solution of a particular problem. Students learning numerical solution of non-linear equations should obtain an intuitive and working understanding of numerical-analysis methods, appreciate the concept of error and the need to analyze and predict it, and develop experience implementing a numerical method using computer software.'),heading('12.1.2 Basic principles of solving non-linear equations in one variable'),paragraph('If f(x) is any continuous function of a single variable x, then any number r for which f(r)=0 is called a root of f(x)=0. We also say r is a zero of f(x).')]
def bisection():
 paths=[];labels=[]
 for j,z in enumerate([2,3]):
  ox=45+j*265
  def pt(x,y):return(ox+57*x,177-39*y)
  paths.extend([path([pt(-.25,0),pt(3.7,0)]),path([pt(0,-2),pt(0,3.7)])]);labels.extend([(ox+213,181,'x'),(ox-9,28,'y'),(ox+64,47,'y=f(x)')])
  fun=lambda x:z-x-.25*x*x
  paths.append(path([pt(i/80,fun(i/80)) for i in range(241)],stroke='#0369a1'))
  r=2*(math.sqrt(1+z)-1)
  for x,key in [(0,'a₀'),(1.5,'c₀'),(3,'b₀')]:
   xx,yy=pt(x,fun(x));xx0,yy0=pt(x,0);paths.append(path([(xx,yy),(xx0,yy0)],dash=True));labels.extend([(xx0-6,yy0+22,key),(xx+5,yy-8,'('+key+',f'+key[0]+'₀)')])
  xx,yy=pt(r,0);labels.append((xx-12,yy+40,'(r,0)'))
 return geometry('Figure 12.1','Figure 12.1 — two schematic bisection cases: sign-changing bracket and midpoint left or right of the root',paths,labels,size=(560,310))
ROOT_CONT=[paragraph('If f(x) is any algebraic function (non-linear equation), actual roots can be found by direct rules such as quadratic formula, common factors and synthetic division. For example the quadratic x²+5x+6=0 has roots r₁=−2,r₂=−3:'),eq(r'f(x)=x^2+5x+6=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x^2+2x+3x+6=0'),eq(r'(x+2)(x+3)=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x+2=0,\ x+3=0\quad\Rightarrow\quad x=-2=r_1,\ x=-3=r_2'),paragraph('On the other hand, the actual roots of non-algebraic equation x²+5x+6=0 are not possible by applying quadratic formula. The only way is to find approximate roots by numerical procedures, bracketing methods and iterative methods.'),heading('12.1.3 Real roots of non-linear equation in one variable'),paragraph('Bisection and regula-falsi are bracketing methods depending on two initial approximations forming a closed interval [a,b]. The values f(a),f(b) must have opposite signs for showing continuity. Once the interval is found, iteration continues until an approximate root is obtained. Iteration is repetition of the process until an answer is achieved.'),heading('(a) Bisection Method'),paragraph('If y=f(x) is continuous on [a,b], it crosses the x-axis at a point (r,0), whose x-coordinate r is a root somewhere in [a,b], as shown in Figure 12.1.'),bisection(),paragraph('Bisection moves the endpoints closer until a small-width interval brackets r. Choose midpoint:'),eq(r'c=\frac{a+b}{2}'),paragraph('Analyze three possibilities: 1. If f(a),f(c) have opposite signs, the root lies in [a,c]; discard b. 2. If f(c),f(b) have opposite signs, the root lies in [c,b]; discard a. 3. If f(c)=0, c is an approximate root to the actual root.'),paragraph('In cases 1 or 2 the new interval is half as wide, squeezing down on the root. Relabel the smaller interval and repeat nested intervals and midpoints. The detailed procedure follows.'),note('The source repeats the same quadratic while calling it non-algebraic; x²+5x+6 remains algebraic and exactly solvable. Many algebraic equations lack simple elementary root formulas, while some non-algebraic equations have exact solutions. Opposite endpoint signs do not show continuity: continuity is a separate prerequisite. With continuity and f(a)f(b)<0, the intermediate value theorem guarantees at least one root, not necessarily a unique one. For instance 1/x changes sign across zero but is discontinuous and has no root. If f(c)=0 exactly, c is an exact root; a small computed residual alone is not a certified root error. The two native diagrams are symbolic schematics, not plots of a specified source equation.')]
